And for some strange reason, they just love it.

235
00:19:08,440 --> 00:19:17,920
Extraordinary people have a dining room table at which they sit and dine, you know, eat at dinner or breakfast or lunch or something.

236
00:19:18,840 --> 00:19:33,560
Extraordinary people have a dining room table that is covered with books and handouts and photocopies and bags of craft supplies and camping gear and the things that the scouts left at the last meeting.

237
00:19:33,560 --> 00:19:42,060
Extraordinary folks make sure that their children are happy and well-educated and prepared for adulthood.

238
00:19:42,740 --> 00:19:45,379
And extraordinary folks do this too for their children.

239
00:19:46,020 --> 00:19:52,020
But they also make sure that the other people's children are happy and well-educated and prepared for adulthood.

240
00:19:53,000 --> 00:20:01,600
Ordinary folks, if they get a little extra money, they might go get a new suit of clothes or a big plasma TV or maybe even a slick two-seater sports car.

241
00:20:01,600 --> 00:20:12,179
Extraordinary folks, if they get a little bit of extra money, might get a new scout uniform or a new sleeping bag or a tent, you know, or maybe a backpack.

242
00:20:12,899 --> 00:20:20,320
Or maybe they'll just grab some popsicle sticks and glitter and glue and pipe cleaners and magic markers and construction paper and some scissors and, you know.

243
00:20:20,320 --> 00:20:34,860
Most of the time, extraordinary folks have to pass up the slick two-seater sports car and coax another year or two out of the beat-up old minivan or pickup truck because, you know, it'll carry more gear or more scouts.

244
00:20:35,500 --> 00:20:36,939
Or, you know, a little bit of both.

245
00:20:38,820 --> 00:20:41,040
Ordinary husbands and wives get to go out to dinner.

246
00:20:42,399 --> 00:20:45,480
And extraordinary husbands and wives get to go out to dinner occasionally.

247
00:20:45,480 --> 00:20:54,060
But ordinary husbands and wives get to go out to dinner where there's no speaker or program or gangs of sugar-crazed Cub Scouts running around.

248
00:20:54,840 --> 00:20:56,640
They go away on romantic weekends.

249
00:20:57,580 --> 00:21:00,460
And they even have family vacations, these ordinary folks.

250
00:21:01,720 --> 00:21:09,660
But extraordinary husbands and wives would consider a blue and gold banquet or a district dinner or a council dinner or an OA banquet to be a date.

251
00:21:10,900 --> 00:21:12,360
You know what I'm talking about, right?

252
00:21:12,360 --> 00:21:15,719
Their weekend trips are anything but romantic.

253
00:21:16,520 --> 00:21:22,560
And any family vacation usually involves a really big family that cooks over on fire and lives in tents.

254
00:21:24,439 --> 00:21:28,159
That is some extraordinary stuff.

255
00:21:28,939 --> 00:21:30,500
There's no shame in being ordinary.

256
00:21:30,659 --> 00:21:34,340
The world needs lots of good, steady, ordinary people to keep things moving along.

257
00:21:34,340 --> 00:21:42,360
But the world would be a much less interesting place without the extraordinary people who freely give themselves to a noble cause like Scouting.

258
00:21:43,139 --> 00:21:48,280
I gotta guess, if you're listening to this podcast, you're one of the extraordinary people.

259
00:21:49,659 --> 00:21:52,600
Now, people aren't lining up to give you a handshake and say thank you.

260
00:21:53,159 --> 00:21:55,080
But here's a little pat on the back from me.

261
00:21:56,020 --> 00:21:57,260
Thanks for being a Scouter.

262
00:21:57,260 --> 00:22:27,240
Thank you.

